 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 4.08,
   "text": "How quickly would the oceans drain if a circular portal 20 meters in diameter appeared at the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 4.08,
   "end": 7.28,
   "text": "bottom of the Challenger Deep, which is the deepest spot in the ocean? Cool.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 7.8,
   "end": 11.6,
   "text": "Where's the other end of this portal? Ivo, we put it directly above the Mars Curiosity rover.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 11.94,
   "end": 16.42,
   "text": "That way, it can finally find incontrovertible evidence of liquid water on Mars' surface.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 16.96,
   "end": 20.78,
   "text": "So what happens back on Earth when the portal opens? Not much. It would take hundreds of",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 20.78,
   "end": 24.28,
   "text": "thousands of years for the ocean to drain. Even though the opening is wider than a basketball",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 24.28,
   "end": 28.28,
   "text": "court, and the water is being forced through at incredible speeds, the oceans are huge.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.086,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 28.28,
   "end": 32.62,
   "text": "The water level would drop by much less than a centimeter per day. There wouldn't even be a cool",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 32.62,
   "end": 36.3,
   "text": "whirlpool on the surface, because the opening is just too small and the ocean is too deep.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 36.74,
   "end": 39.96,
   "text": "But let's suppose we speed up the draining by opening more or bigger drains at the bottom",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 39.96,
   "end": 43.92,
   "text": "of Challenger Deep. Here's how the world looks at the start, and here's the map after the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 43.92,
   "end": 48.68,
   "text": "oceans drop 50 meters. Great Britons, Sri Lanka, New Guinea, Java, and Borneo are all now connected",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 48.68,
   "end": 53.3,
   "text": "to their neighbors. And after 2,000 years of trying to hold back the sea, the Netherlands is",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 53.3,
   "end": 58.02,
   "text": "finally high and dry. When the sea level reaches minus 100 meters, the Black Sea stops shrinking",
   "avg_logprob": -0.078,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.006
  },
  {
   "start": 58.02,
   "end": 62.22,
   "text": "because it's no longer connected to the ocean. By 200 meters of sea level fall, the map is really",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 62.22,
   "end": 66.76,
   "text": "starting to look weird. New islands are appearing. Indonesia is a big blob. The Netherlands now",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 66.76,
   "end": 71.18,
   "text": "controls much of Europe. At 500 meters, Japan becomes a causeway that connects the Korean",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 71.18,
   "end": 74.9,
   "text": "peninsula with Russia. After the top kilometer of the oceans have drained, New Zealand,",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 75.1,
   "end": 78.9,
   "text": "which appropriately was named after a province in the Netherlands, continues to grow dramatically.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 79.56,
   "end": 83.86,
   "text": "Meanwhile, the old Zealanders cross the land bridge into North America. By 5 kilometers,",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 84.14,
   "end": 87.82,
   "text": "most of the major oceans have become disconnected and stop draining. When the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.066,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 87.82,
   "end": 91.24,
   "text": "empties, there is a surprising amount of water left, although a lot of it consists of very",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  },
  {
   "start": 91.24,
   "end": 94.84,
   "text": "shallow seas, with a few trenches where the water might be as deep as 4 or 5 kilometers.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  },
  {
   "start": 95.58,
   "end": 99.2,
   "text": "Vacuuming up so much of the ocean would massively alter the climate and ecosystems",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  },
  {
   "start": 99.2,
   "end": 102.6,
   "text": "in ways that are hard to predict. At the very least, it would almost certainly involve",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  },
  {
   "start": 102.6,
   "end": 106.88,
   "text": "a collapse of the biosphere and mass extinctions at every level. But it's possible, if unlikely,",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  },
  {
   "start": 107.2,
   "end": 108.7,
   "text": "that humans could manage to survive.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.07,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.016
  }
 ],
